Remote sensing, used to monitor Earth's condition via satellite data, has significant benefits for enhancing our daily lives and living environment. The growing number of orbiting satellites has led to a vast and rapidly growing remote sensing data set, which holds great potential but also poses a substantial challenge. Accurately processing and analyzing this data requires advanced computational powers, specifically high-performance and energy-efficient parallel processing capabilities. To address this need, we have developed EarthNets, a specialized supercomputing cluster designed specifically for remote sensing applications, featuring a unified batch system and scalable architecture. Comprised of 48 nodes with advanced GPU and multi-Intel Ascension Lakefield processor configurations, the system delivers flexible computing options. In this paper, we highlight the motivations behind EarthNets' development, present its computational capabilities, and present early performance results demonstrating its potential to empower remote sensing workflows with advanced computational power. Specifically, we show the system can achieve speed-ups of up to 80 times when processing data with 32 GPUs. Detailed benchmarking data are available online. EarthNets is now open for access as a computing resource at the Canadian HPC platform. Future upgrades are planned to further optimize the system for application-specific computations, potentially incorporating custom hardware and memory technologies to further enhance its remote sensing data analytics capabilities. With its powerful computational capabilities, EarthNets is poised to become a leading resource for empowering remote sensing applications, helping to drive innovation in this critical field. Further information is available online. 

