We conducted an exhaustive examination comparing the statistical properties of non-hierarchical three-body systems with corresponding theoretical models. By running and analyzing one million simulations for diverse initial conditions, both with and without equal masses in three-body systems, we were able to achieve precise statistical accuracy. Four distinct measures were used to characterize the distribution of ergodic probabilities: the skewness, kurtosis, excess kurtosis, and tail weight. The ergodic probability was found to be a reliable indicator of system dynamics, and its distribution was consistent across different initial configurations. The theoretical models based on flux and energy correlations showed excellent agreement with our experimental results, and these statistical measures were instrumental in validating the predictions made by these models. Furthermore, the statistical measures were able to capture the distinct behavioral trends of the three-body systems associated with different masses, and the results highlighted the significance of taking into account the effects of mass variations in astrophysical contexts. Ultimately, the outstanding performance of the theoretical models suggests that statistical mechanics can be effectively applied to the complex three-body systems, and future research will focus on leveraging this approach to gain greater understanding of these systems and to inform hierarchical multiple-body problem models. Tables of statistical moments and associated error bars, along with the custom-made Octave script used to generate the figures, are available online. The associated physical review E-Submission has been reviewed and approved. 

