\section{Introduction}
Three-body systems are fundamental to astronomy and astrophysics, and their study is crucial for understanding various celestial phenomena. By examining statistical properties of these systems under different initial conditions, researchers can gain insights into their distribution in astrophysical environments. A novel approach has been developed to analyze these systems using statistical mechanics, and the outcome shows that this method accurately represents the ergodic probability distribution of non-hierarchical three-body systems. Theoretical models based on flux and energy correlations exhibit excellent agreement with experimental results, and several statistical measures are introduced to characterize the ergodic probability distribution. The findings of this research reveal distinct behavioral patterns associated with different masses and provide a statistical basis for predicting three-body system dynamics. The outcome of this study suggests that statistical mechanics can be effectively applied to the complex three-body systems, and future research will focus on leveraging this approach to inform hierarchical multiple-body problem models. 

